  "articleBody": "Getting your website crawled and indexed correctly can feel like a maze, and it's easy to make a few wrong turns. Avoiding these common mistakes can save you a lot of headache and ensure your content actually gets seen by search engines and, more importantly, by potential customers.\n\n- Accidentally disallowing important paths in `robots.txt` — how to avoid it: Always double-check your `robots.txt` file before deploying changes. Use Google Search Console's `robots.txt` Tester to confirm that the paths you want crawled are indeed allowed, especially for new sections or critical service pages.\n\n- Using `robots.txt` when the objective is deindexing — how to avoid it: Remember, `robots.txt` prevents crawling, not indexing. If you want a page to disappear from search results, use a `noindex` meta tag or HTTP header. `robots.txt` can actually *prevent* Google from seeing the `noindex` directive, trapping the page in search results.\n\n- Leaving `noindex` on production pages — how to avoid it: It sounds obvious, but it happens! Often, `noindex` tags are used during development and forgotten before launch. Implement a staging environment check to ensure all `noindex` tags are removed from pages intended for public search once they go live.\n\n- Listing redirected, canonicalized, broken, or non-indexable URLs in sitemaps — how to avoid it: Your XML sitemap should be a clean list of only the canonical, indexable URLs you want search engines to find. Regularly audit your sitemap to remove any pages that are no longer active, redirecting, or are marked `noindex` or `noarchive`.\n\n- Relying on JavaScript links or screens without crawlable URLs — how to avoid it: While modern search engines are good at rendering JavaScript, relying solely on JavaScript to display critical content or navigation can create crawlability gaps. Ensure that core content and links are accessible in a crawlable HTML format, or verify with Google Search Console's live test that JS content is rendered.\n\n- Failing to verify that Googlebot can access required resources — how to avoid it: Your pages might load for users, but Googlebot might struggle with CSS, JavaScript, or images that make up your page. Use the URL Inspection tool in Google Search Console to check resource loading. If Googlebot can't see crucial elements, it might misinterpret your page's content or quality.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps: When you're dealing with a large site, complex JavaScript applications, or migrations that affect thousands of URLs, bringing in a specialist can be a game-changer. A single error in `robots.txt` or a template can impact vast sections of your site, and an expert can quickly pinpoint and fix these issues before they become major visibility problems.",
